now to the investigation into a massive  explosion in a southern indiana  neighborhood that killed at least three  people the blast yesterday damaged 39  homes fire official says 11 of them are  inhabitable the explosion happened in  evansville which is about 150 miles  southwest of indianapolis rescue teams  say there could be more victims trapped  in the wreckage nikki batiste has the  story  surveillance video captures the moment  of the explosion smoke billowing into  the sky  also caught on ring video from across  the street  officials say debris was strewn 100 feet  in all directions this is the aftermath  two people confirmed killed in the house  that exploded another in the home next  door we have eight different agencies on  scene including  alcohol tobacco and firearms the cause  is still under investigation cbs news  obtained these photos from a man living  across the street from the explosion  they show not only what's left of the  houses that were destroyed but the  impact the blast had inside his own home  dozens of homes suffered extensive  damage another nearby resident drew  straiter shot this video and says he  feels lucky to be alive i was just  hanging out on the couch watching some  tv and  window come in right behind me and blue  glass on my hair some of the damaged  homes were so unstable search and rescue  efforts had to be called off overnight  the structures aren't safe to enter so  there may be somebody still in there the  local energy company says it's working  closely with officials as they tried to  determine the cause of the blast  for cbs morning's i'm nikki batiste
 

